# Airline Commercial Strategist Brief

**Run:** quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2
**Question I test the thesis against:** What will the carriers do in response?
**One-line answer:** The acoustics are the easy part. The politically load-bearing question is who owns the boarding call and who pays for the reverb time — and both of those live inside the 2025 Use & Lease, not in the design manual.

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **Boarding calls belong to the airline, not to MWAA.** At every U.S. mainline hub, the gate-side PA microphone is operated by an airline agent under an airline procedure manual. A quiet-by-design standard that specifies acoustics, PA infrastructure, and zoning is inside MWAA's authority. A standard that dictates *whether* boarding is called overhead, in what script, and with what frequency is not — it is a term the signatory carriers will treat as an operating-procedures matter and will contest accordingly. That distinction should be codified explicitly in the standard before it goes to the Board.
2. **The 2025 Use & Lease is a hybrid-residual, 140% coverage, $9 B CCP contract with an MII regime that has not been publicly disclosed.** Every incremental dollar of acoustic treatment, higher-NC mechanical selection, or denser PA loudspeaker layout raises airline rates under the residual model. The MII vote — present in 36 of the 66 largest U.S. airports — is the mechanism by which signatory carriers can slow or block capital scope funded through rates and charges [Source: https://www.gao.gov/products/rced-91-39]. If MWAA cannot show whether the standard triggers MII or falls under a design-manual carve-out, it does not have a deliverable strategy.
3. **CPE headroom exists at IAD but is being spent.** IAD signatory CPE is currently $12.77 and the 2025A Official Statement forecasts it rising to roughly $20.71 by 2030 under the existing $6.99 B IAD CCP, before the incremental $20 B–$22.5 B United/USDOT program announced in July 2026 is layered on [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. A quiet-by-design premium of even 1–3% of terminal fit-out cost is defensible pre-design; it is not defensible as a post-award change order once IAD CPE is inside the airline peer-benchmark trigger zone.
4. **DCA is a slot-constrained, high-turn, single-runway operation. Its economics do not tolerate a silence-first hold room.** A denied boarding costs an airline between $2,300 and $16,000 depending on how it is handled [Source: https://www.iata.org/en/publications/newsletters/iata-knowledge-hub/the-cost-of-a-denied-boarding-is-more-than-you-think/]. Block-minute cost for a U.S. narrowbody is roughly $100 per minute [Source: https://www.airlines.org/dataset/u-s-passenger-carrier-delay-costs/]. A missed slot at DCA — because a passenger did not hear boarding — cascades into a departure delay that is worth more than the same delay at almost any other U.S. airport, because DCA slots are federally scarce and there is no runway alternative. That is the constraint that must be written into the standard, or the standard will be written for MWAA by American Airlines after the first missed-boarding incident.
5. **United has more leverage over the IAD standard than any single airport-side stakeholder.** United carries the majority of IAD enplanements, holds Concourse E under a long-term lease signed in parallel with the 2025 Use & Lease, and is the named private partner in the July 2026 $20 B–$22.5 B expansion announcement [Source: https://united.mediaroom.com/2026-07-29-Americas-Newest-Capital-Gateway-Unveiled-More-Than-20-Billion-Transformation-at-Washington-Dulles-International-Airport]. Any quiet-by-design standard that touches gate-hold operations, PA scripting, or Polaris/UnitedClub interior acoustics is a term United will price into the still-open financing negotiations for the incremental scope. MWAA's optimal move is to set the standard before those negotiations conclude, not after.
6. **American at DCA is not a passive counterparty either.** American holds roughly a 53% share at DCA [Source: https://simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/] and treats DCA as one of its most profitable hubs by margin per departure despite being its smallest. A standard that appears in the design manual without American's operational buy-in on the Terminal 2 hold rooms will be treated as an implementation risk by the carrier and a political risk by the board.
7. **The dehubbing case studies teach one lesson relevant to this run: airports lose leverage over their standards when their anchor carriers stop concurring on capital.** No hub was dehubbed over a PA policy. But CVG in 2005–2013, PIT after 2004, STL after 2003, and CLE after 2014 all show the same sequence: the carrier signals cost pressure, the airport hardens capital commitments the carrier is no longer willing to underwrite, and the carrier withdraws the traffic that made the standard affordable [Source: https://airlinegeeks.com/2017/06/14/the-death-and-rebirth-of-memphis-mem-and-cincinnati-cvg/]. MWAA is not being dehubbed. But the mechanism — an airport writing standards ahead of what its carriers will fund — is the same. A quiet standard has to survive the 2039 Use & Lease renewal.
8. **The under-recognised commercial upside is concession revenue, not passenger comfort.** London City's 2008 silent-terminal policy and the peer literature on airport retail attribute measurable dwell-time and spend increases to lower cognitive load in the departures lounge [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. Non-aeronautical revenue flows to MWAA under the residual model, offsetting airline CPE. This is the argument that turns the carriers from skeptics into supporters at the MII table, and it should be quantified before the standard is drafted, not after.

