## 2. Evidence

### Airline industry structure as it applies to MWAA

Post-consolidation, the U.S. mainline market is a four-carrier oligopoly (American, Delta, United, Southwest) plus a small ULCC segment (Spirit, Frontier, Allegiant). MWAA has full exposure to that structure: United dominates IAD, American dominates DCA, and Delta/Southwest/Alaska/JetBlue/Air Canada hold subordinate positions at DCA. The two dominant carriers each have the concentration necessary to block or shape any airport-funded capital scope routed through MII [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airport-finance/articles/airport-capital-review].

The 2008–2016 merger wave is directly relevant to how the standard will be received. Delta/Northwest, United/Continental, and American/US Airways each produced a period in which the merged carrier reevaluated its network and thinned or closed hubs (CVG, MEM, CLE). At each of those airports, the airport authority had recently made large capital commitments that were then stranded [Source: https://simpleflying.com/what-happened-delta-air-lines-hub-cincinnati/]. The relevant question for MWAA is not whether it will be dehubbed — it will not — but whether the standard it writes today will still be affordable if United's transatlantic strategy at IAD shifts in the 2035–2039 window.

### Hub-and-spoke economics as they apply to this thesis

IAD is a genuine hub — connecting bank structure, wide-body international feed, and international joint-venture gateway status through United's Star Alliance and its transatlantic JV with Lufthansa Group and Air Canada. The July 2026 announcement explicitly positions IAD as United's transatlantic gateway [Source: https://united.mediaroom.com/2026-07-29-Americas-Newest-Capital-Gateway-Unveiled-More-Than-20-Billion-Transformation-at-Washington-Dulles-International-Airport]. Hub economics have two implications for the quiet standard. First, international connecting passengers spend materially longer in the terminal than O&D passengers, which raises the value of low-cognitive-load design. Second, banked operations create synchronised peaks of gate-side PA usage — the noise problem the standard is trying to solve is worst during connecting banks, which is exactly when a residual airline-funded standard is easiest to sell to the carrier.

DCA is not a hub in the network-planning sense. American treats it as a slot-protected high-margin operation, not a connecting complex [Source: https://simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-national-international-flights/]. This matters because the operational rhythm at DCA is O&D-heavy, high-turn, and slot-anchored. Silence-first policies were designed at airports (LCY, HEL, CPH) with structurally different traffic mixes. Direct transfer of practice from those airports to DCA is what the run prompt explicitly asks the Council to interrogate.

### Cost per enplanement and airline benchmarks

Current signatory CPE: IAD $12.77, DCA $10.41 [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/dca-slot-expansion-obbba]. MWAA's own airport-finance disclosures separately show DCA aeronautical revenue per enplanement at approximately $7.49 in FY2025, which is a narrower measurement (aero revenue only, not full signatory CPE) [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airports/DCA]. Either measure puts MWAA below the airline-benchmark trigger zone for network diversion (broadly $20+ for large hubs).

CPE headroom is finite. The 2025A Official Statement projects IAD CPE at approximately $20.71 by 2030 under the $9 B CCP alone [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. The incremental Dulles transformation announced with United and USDOT in July 2026 is not fully financed in the public record. Any additional acoustic-treatment cost that is not offset by non-aero revenue or federal grant funding flows through to CPE, and CPE is the single number airline network planners use to compare hubs.

### Use agreements: residual, MII, and the design-manual boundary

MWAA's 2025–2039 Use & Lease is a two-airport hybrid-residual structure with a 140% airline-funded debt-service coverage target [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. Residual structures make the airline the residual bearer of cost — attractive for MWAA's credit (Moody's Aa3, S&P AA-, Fitch AA- as of May 2025) and precisely the reason airlines guard MII rights so carefully.

MII clauses typically require both a majority of signatories by number and a majority by activity (landed weight or enplanements) [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/how-us-airports-are-regulated]. At IAD, United alone can approach the activity-majority threshold; at DCA, American does. Whether the current agreement's MII regime is triggered by design-manual amendments (as opposed to line-item capital projects) is a specific term that is not disclosed publicly and that MWAA counsel needs to answer before this standard is scoped.

Common MII exemptions — projects majority-funded by federal grant, projects required by federal/state safety regulation, and safety-and-security projects — offer legitimate paths for MWAA to route the parts of the quiet-by-design standard that align with NFPA 72 voice-evacuation intelligibility, ADA accessible-information compliance, and TSA/CBP mandated communication [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/how-us-airports-are-regulated]. In other words, the standard's compliance-envelope framing (NFPA/ADA/ACAA) is not just legally necessary. It is the political move that keeps most of the standard outside MII.

### Route profitability, missed-boarding cost, and the DCA constraint

A denied boarding costs a carrier $2,300 to $16,000 [Source: https://www.iata.org/en/publications/newsletters/iata-knowledge-hub/the-cost-of-a-denied-boarding-is-more-than-you-think/]. A U.S. narrowbody block minute is roughly $100 and can rise to $300 [Source: https://www.airlines.org/dataset/u-s-passenger-carrier-delay-costs/]. Both numbers are systematically higher at DCA because a missed slot cannot be recovered — DCA is capped at 67 hourly slots [Source: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12504.html], its slot base was frozen from 2012 through 2024, and the 2024 beyond-perimeter exemptions cost their carriers a documented commitment to launch service by March 2025 [Source: https://aviationa2z.com/index.php/2024/10/17/five-us-airlines-awarded-new-washington-reagan-slots/]. Under the 2025 Use & Lease, MWAA earns $1 million per new beyond-perimeter flight pair from DCA net remaining revenue — but only if DCA maintains a $2 CPE advantage over IAD [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/dca-slot-expansion-obbba]. Any DCA acoustic-treatment premium that closes that gap has revenue consequences MWAA finance has already contracted for.

The operational implication is unambiguous. A boarding call at a DCA gate for a beyond-perimeter departure that misses its slot because a passenger was in a "quiet zone" and did not hear the call is a chargeable incident. The standard has to specify how boarding is communicated at DCA, and it cannot rely solely on FIDS, mobile push, and visual paging without an audible fallback in the hold room.

