## 3. Case studies — carrier responses to airport passenger-experience decisions

**Cincinnati (2005–2013).** Delta reduced CVG from 600 daily departures to fewer than 180 across the period, driven by Comair pilot-strike cost inflation, 50-seat regional-jet economics, and Delta's own bankruptcy [Source: https://simpleflying.com/what-happened-delta-air-lines-hub-cincinnati/]. The airport had recently invested heavily in Concourse B and international infrastructure. Public airline messaging cited network optimisation; the route economics showed that CVG was uncompetitive on a cost-per-ASM basis against Delta's Atlanta operation once the pilot contract reset the cost base. Relevance to this run: airports that lock in high fixed-cost standards without a carrier concurrence path expose themselves in the next network review.

**Pittsburgh (2004).** US Airways progressively withdrew from PIT after opening the new midfield terminal in 1992. The airport authority had underwritten a facility sized to a hub the carrier no longer needed post-bankruptcy. CPE spiked as PIT tried to recover fixed cost across a smaller base. The lesson MWAA has already internalised: the 2025 Use & Lease residual structure exists precisely to prevent this dynamic recurring.

**London City (from 2008).** The best-documented commercial case for silence-first policy — sustained since 2008 across two ownership regimes — is a small, single-runway, business-heavy operation with a homogeneous passenger profile [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. LCY's boarding calls were eliminated except for gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure. LCY's passenger volume, journey length, and carrier mix bear almost no resemblance to IAD. It bears a partial resemblance to DCA Terminal 2 in business-mix terms only. The run prompt is correct to say practice cannot transfer directly. What can transfer is the compliance framework: define the exception categories (irregular ops, gate change, FIDS failure, safety) rather than the elimination.

**Helsinki (adopted 2015).** HEL removed non-safety boarding announcements and defaulted to FIDS plus Finnair CARE ambassadors. Publicly available operational data on missed boardings and irregular-operations handling is not disclosed in a form suitable for direct benchmarking. The absence of published outcome data is itself a finding: MWAA cannot cite HEL as proof of concept without pushing Finnair or Finavia for measured results. Any inference otherwise is branding, not evidence.

**Concourse E at IAD (opening late 2026).** Concourse E — 435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, 40,000-sq-ft United Club, direct AeroTrain access — is being delivered under a $500M+ program with more than $200M in federal funding [Source: https://www.travelmarketreport.com/articles/%20%20%20%20/air/articles/united-airlines-invests-in-new-500m-concourse-at-washington-dulles]. Design is fixed. The quiet-by-design standard cannot govern Concourse E. It can, however, be commissioned against Concourse E as the reference case for the next facility. If MWAA does not measure Concourse E acoustic performance in the six months after opening, it will have no baseline for what the standard should require of Concourses C and D.

