## 5. The strategist's veto question

The Council will be tempted to write a standard that reads well in a design manual. The test I apply is narrower: does the standard survive an MII vote, a United gate-hold operational review, an American Terminal 2 hold-room walkthrough, and the 2030–2035 airline benchmarking cycle in which network planners will compare IAD CPE to ATL, ORD, and BOS?

If any of the four fails, the standard fails.

The best-shaped standard for MWAA is one that (a) is anchored in NFPA 72, ADA, and ACAA compliance envelopes to sit outside MII, (b) prescribes physical-infrastructure performance (NC/RC targets, RT60, STI, PA zoning and spillover, loudspeaker density) rather than airline operating procedure, (c) preserves airline discretion over boarding-call scripting with a required audible-fallback capability at slot-constrained gates, and (d) makes Concourse E the baseline for measured commissioning of the next facility. That standard the carriers will sign. A standard framed as "quiet" — as a policy about announcements rather than a policy about the built environment — will be renegotiated at MWAA's expense the first time a missed slot at DCA or a Polaris passenger complaint at IAD gives United or American the opening.

